  "query_preview": "..\n(This afternoon through next Wednesday)\nIssued at 1159 AM CDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nA relatively benign weather pattern is ongoing across the Mid-\nSouth with temperatures spanning the 80s. A frontal boundary is\nslowly progressing through the area, with ongoing cloudiness\ngenerally along and south of the I-40 corridor. As this frontal\nboundary continues to push south, some isolated showers and\nthunderstorms could fire into the afternoon hours. However, there\ndoes appear to be a decent amount of dry air aloft, which will\nlikely inhibit most of this activity. Otherwise, high\ntemperatures will remain just above-normal by the afternoon.\nLocations further north will be closer to normal in the upper\n80s, with low to mid 90s elsewhere. Into the overnight hours,\nclear skies coupled with light winds and generally low dew point\ndepressions may allow some patchy fog to develop. While\nconfidence is low, if this were to occur, locations close to the\nTN River have the best chance. Any fog that develops near sunrise\nwill quickly mix out into Friday. Following the aforementioned\nfrontal boundary, high temperatures on Friday will dip back down\na few degrees closer to normal, with values in the upper 80s to\nlow 90s. With the frontal boundary expected to be south of the\narea by this point, dry conditions will likely prevail across the\nentire area.\n\nBy Saturday, an upper-level ridge will continue to push across\nthe Central Plains, eventually moving over the region into the\nfirst half of next week. With this building ridge and\ncorresponding surface high developing across the southeast,\nexpect for a dry and warm forecast to persist for the foreseeable\nfuture. For Saturday, high temperatures may still linger in the\nlow to mid 90s. By Sunday, predominantly southerly flow will\nreturn, allowing for temperatures to climb further into the mid\nto upper 90s. Despite the southerly flow regime, dew point\ntemperatures may remain somewhat manageable. There is currently a\nlow probability (around 10 to 20%) of some locations across the\nDelta regions exceeding 105 degrees, thus needing heat headlines.\nThis will be something to watch into the latter half of the\nforecast period. However, given the low confidence, generally\nplan to message the rising temperatures at this point.",
